WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.934 --> 00:00:02.602
Back now from Saint John's, Newfoundland,

00:00:02.602 --> 00:00:03.903
where the submersible titan

00:00:03.903 --> 00:00:05.071
first went out to sea

00:00:05.071 --> 00:00:06.406
on its way to the Titanic.

00:00:06.406 --> 00:00:07.140
We're talking tonight

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:08.408
about the five lives lost,

00:00:08.408 --> 00:00:09.709
also about the vessel itself.

00:00:09.709 --> 00:00:11.311
And the questions surrounding it,

00:00:11.311 --> 00:00:12.612
namely the decision to build it

00:00:12.612 --> 00:00:13.113
out of carbon

00:00:13.113 --> 00:00:14.848
composites and non-certified safety

00:00:14.848 --> 00:00:16.316
as other such vessels are.

00:00:16.316 --> 00:00:17.017
Back now with Deep-Sea

00:00:17.017 --> 00:00:18.184
Explorer and Titanic director

00:00:18.184 --> 00:00:20.286
James Cameron

00:00:20.787 --> 00:00:22.155
I mean, it's extraordinary to me

00:00:22.155 --> 00:00:23.323
that this debris field

00:00:23.323 --> 00:00:23.923
is just

00:00:23.923 --> 00:00:25.759
600 feet away

00:00:25.759 --> 00:00:28.094
from the bow of the Titanic.

00:00:28.161 --> 00:00:28.661
Obviously,

00:00:28.661 --> 00:00:29.162
people think

00:00:29.162 --> 00:00:30.330
about the barrel of the Titanic

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:31.931
and they think of your film Titanic

00:00:31.931 --> 00:00:34.534
with that iconic scene on the bow.

00:00:36.136 --> 00:00:38.371
I heard you earlier to kind of talk

00:00:38.371 --> 00:00:40.240
about these two captains

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:44.444
and a kind of a similarity that you see.

00:00:44.444 --> 00:00:45.712
And I wonder if you could could

00:00:45.712 --> 00:00:47.147
could talk about that.

00:00:47.147 --> 00:00:49.716
I think there's a great almost surreal

00:00:50.183 --> 00:00:53.286
irony here, which is Titanic sank

00:00:53.286 --> 00:00:55.255
because the captain took it

00:00:55.255 --> 00:00:58.558
full steam into an ice feel that night.

00:00:58.558 --> 00:00:59.459
On a moonless night

00:00:59.459 --> 00:01:02.162
with very poor visibility

00:01:02.162 --> 00:01:03.997
after he had been repeatedly warned

00:01:03.997 --> 00:01:06.633
by telegram, by Marconi, Graham by radio

00:01:07.233 --> 00:01:09.169
during the day that that's

00:01:09.169 --> 00:01:10.637
what was ahead of him.

00:01:10.637 --> 00:01:12.472
And so I think we're also seeing

00:01:12.472 --> 00:01:13.306
a parallel here

00:01:13.306 --> 00:01:16.876
with unheeded warnings about a sub

00:01:16.876 --> 00:01:18.078
that was not certified

00:01:18.078 --> 00:01:20.580
where the entire deep submergence

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:21.881
community actually

00:01:21.881 --> 00:01:22.916
or not the entire community,

00:01:22.916 --> 00:01:24.017
but a large number of them

00:01:24.017 --> 00:01:26.586
got together to write a letter to Ocean

00:01:26.586 --> 00:01:27.487
Gate, the company,

00:01:27.487 --> 00:01:29.122
and say we believe

00:01:29.122 --> 00:01:31.157
that this could lead to catastrophe.

00:01:31.591 --> 00:01:33.059
It was less a criticism

00:01:33.059 --> 00:01:34.727
of the engineering

00:01:34.727 --> 00:01:36.629
than of the process,

00:01:36.629 --> 00:01:38.198
but it contemplate that

00:01:38.198 --> 00:01:38.598
the fact

00:01:38.598 --> 00:01:39.332
that the engineering

00:01:39.332 --> 00:01:41.801
probably wouldn't pass muster

00:01:42.669 --> 00:01:45.071
from a certification bureau.

00:01:45.939 --> 00:01:46.840
And so they were trying

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:48.041
to head this whole thing off

00:01:48.041 --> 00:01:49.576
it was our worst nightmare.

00:01:49.576 --> 00:01:50.677
I mean, all of us in the deep

00:01:50.677 --> 00:01:51.711
submergence community,

00:01:51.711 --> 00:01:52.612
people like myself

00:01:52.612 --> 00:01:54.747
that pilot subs and designed subs.

00:01:54.981 --> 00:01:57.083
Implosion is obviously the

00:01:57.350 --> 00:01:58.084
the Specter

00:01:58.084 --> 00:02:00.186
that looms over us all the time.

00:02:00.186 --> 00:02:01.754
But because of that,

00:02:01.754 --> 00:02:03.423
that's the thing that you engineer

00:02:03.423 --> 00:02:06.126
for the most years in advance.

00:02:06.126 --> 00:02:07.861
So that should never be the problem.

00:02:07.861 --> 00:02:10.964
I've never believed that if I was going

00:02:10.964 --> 00:02:12.198
to have a serious problem

00:02:12.198 --> 00:02:14.100
in a sub that it would be implosion,

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:15.902
maybe entanglement in a fishing net,

00:02:15.902 --> 00:02:18.004
maybe a fire from the electronics

00:02:18.004 --> 00:02:20.240
Hard to rule those things out. Implosion.

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:21.508
Absolutely not.

00:02:21.508 --> 00:02:22.976
Especially with modern

00:02:22.976 --> 00:02:25.445
finite element analysis and

00:02:25.445 --> 00:02:26.746
computer aided design.

00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:31.484
Do you.

00:02:31.551 --> 00:02:33.920
I mean, what should be the

00:02:34.787 --> 00:02:36.189
what should we learn from this?

00:02:36.189 --> 00:02:37.724
Because obviously,

00:02:37.724 --> 00:02:39.292
water expert deepwater exploration.

00:02:39.292 --> 00:02:40.660
I mean, there are

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:42.061
there's a pretty great

00:02:42.061 --> 00:02:42.795
I mean, I know if it's great,

00:02:42.795 --> 00:02:44.030
but a very good safety

00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:46.966
record with people who have done

00:02:47.233 --> 00:02:48.368
the certification, people

00:02:48.368 --> 00:02:50.703
who have really studied this.

00:02:50.703 --> 00:02:52.005
Do you worry about this

00:02:52.005 --> 00:02:52.805
having an impact

00:02:52.805 --> 00:02:55.108
on the continued exploration?

00:02:55.108 --> 00:02:55.742
I do.

00:02:55.742 --> 00:02:55.975
I do.

00:02:55.975 --> 00:02:56.309
Look, I'm

00:02:56.309 --> 00:02:57.510
not worried about exploration

00:02:57.510 --> 00:02:59.612
because explorers will go

00:03:00.113 --> 00:03:01.581
and I'm not worried about innovation

00:03:01.581 --> 00:03:03.149
because people will innovate.

00:03:03.149 --> 00:03:05.218
I'm worried that it has a negative impact

00:03:05.218 --> 00:03:05.952
on, let's say,

00:03:05.952 --> 00:03:08.288
citizen explorers, tourists, you know.

00:03:08.521 --> 00:03:09.822
But these are serious people

00:03:09.822 --> 00:03:11.324
with serious curiosity,

00:03:11.324 --> 00:03:12.825
willing to put serious money down

00:03:12.825 --> 00:03:15.328
to go to these interesting places.

00:03:15.328 --> 00:03:17.163
And I don't want to discourage that,

00:03:17.163 --> 00:03:18.965
but I think that it's almost it.

00:03:18.965 --> 00:03:19.832
Now, the lesson

00:03:19.832 --> 00:03:21.534
the takeaway is make sure

00:03:21.534 --> 00:03:22.802
if you're going to go into a vehicle,

00:03:22.802 --> 00:03:24.571
whether it's an aircraft or a surface

00:03:24.571 --> 00:03:26.739
craft or a submersible

00:03:26.739 --> 00:03:27.674
that it's been through,

00:03:27.674 --> 00:03:29.742
a certifying agencies,

00:03:29.742 --> 00:03:31.277
you know, that it's been signed off

00:03:31.277 --> 00:03:31.945
every day.

00:03:31.945 --> 00:03:34.347
We trust our lives to to engineering.

00:03:34.547 --> 00:03:36.049
We step into an elevator.

00:03:36.049 --> 00:03:37.083
We make an assumption

00:03:37.083 --> 00:03:38.251
that somebody somewhere has done

00:03:38.251 --> 00:03:39.385
the math properly

00:03:39.385 --> 00:03:41.254
and it's all been certified properly.

00:03:41.254 --> 00:03:42.956
We should take the same precautions

00:03:42.956 --> 00:03:44.624
when we get into a submersible,

00:03:44.624 --> 00:03:45.892
even if it's at a resort.

00:03:45.892 --> 00:03:48.061
And we're only going down 300 feet

00:03:48.061 --> 00:03:50.396
or a thousand feet.

00:03:50.396 --> 00:03:50.697
You know,

00:03:50.697 --> 00:03:53.266
I'm a I'm a partner in a submersible

00:03:53.266 --> 00:03:54.300
company called Triton.

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:55.501
I'll say that upfront.

00:03:55.501 --> 00:03:57.270
Triton not Titan.

00:03:57.270 --> 00:03:59.973
And Triton has a perfect operational

00:03:59.973 --> 00:04:01.407
safety record across

00:04:01.407 --> 00:04:04.344
20 vehicles and 10000 hours

00:04:04.344 --> 00:04:04.978
of people

00:04:04.978 --> 00:04:07.480
diving to depths up to a thousand meters.

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:10.516
It can be done, but it requires rigor.

00:04:10.783 --> 00:04:11.251
And I think

00:04:11.251 --> 00:04:13.686
all of us in the community now

00:04:13.686 --> 00:04:16.589
now our worst fears have have happened.

00:04:16.623 --> 00:04:18.324
And we know why it happened.

00:04:18.324 --> 00:04:19.993
I think largely, you know,

00:04:19.993 --> 00:04:23.596
it puts us now on even more alert to be

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:25.098
to be disciplined

00:04:25.098 --> 00:04:26.132
and to really think

00:04:26.132 --> 00:04:28.067
about the ethics of it.

00:04:28.067 --> 00:04:29.569
You know, there's a lot of countries

00:04:29.569 --> 00:04:31.471
where subs are diving all over the world.

00:04:31.471 --> 00:04:32.238
You're not going to have

00:04:32.238 --> 00:04:33.906
regulation everywhere that solves

00:04:33.906 --> 00:04:34.974
this problem.

00:04:34.974 --> 00:04:35.775
It's really more

00:04:35.775 --> 00:04:37.510
that we have to just accept

00:04:37.510 --> 00:04:39.512
a standard of practice

00:04:39.512 --> 00:04:40.113
that we

00:04:40.113 --> 00:04:43.116
that we don't encourage operators to

00:04:43.116 --> 00:04:46.152
to work without without proper ads

00:04:46.152 --> 00:04:48.087
or German, Lloyd's or whatever

00:04:48.087 --> 00:04:49.789
it is, certification

00:04:52.759 --> 00:04:54.060
that the you know,

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:54.560
the Coast

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:55.528
Guard rear admiral

00:04:55.528 --> 00:04:56.029
today was talking

00:04:56.029 --> 00:04:57.630
about the unforgiving environment

00:04:57.630 --> 00:04:58.598
at those depths.

00:04:58.598 --> 00:05:00.366
Can you just talk about that

00:05:00.366 --> 00:05:02.468
feeling of being down there?

00:05:02.468 --> 00:05:02.702
I mean,

00:05:02.702 --> 00:05:03.870
I can't believe

00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:05.972
how deep you have gone into the ocean

00:05:06.139 --> 00:05:08.141
three times deeper than the Titanic,

00:05:08.141 --> 00:05:09.375
I think you said. Yeah.

00:05:09.375 --> 00:05:11.978
What is that feeling of being down there?

00:05:11.978 --> 00:05:12.679
Well, I always say

00:05:12.679 --> 00:05:14.113
you'd have to take a backhoe with you

00:05:14.113 --> 00:05:14.714
to go deeper

00:05:14.714 --> 00:05:17.950
than I went anywhere on this planet.

00:05:17.950 --> 00:05:20.320
That is a feeling of of remoteness.

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:22.789
I knew when I made that dove

00:05:22.789 --> 00:05:24.123
that there was no hope of rescue.

00:05:24.123 --> 00:05:24.724
There literally

00:05:24.724 --> 00:05:26.192
was no vehicle in the world,

00:05:26.192 --> 00:05:27.460
no matter if you could fly it

00:05:27.460 --> 00:05:27.960
in, no matter

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:28.861
if I could survive

00:05:28.861 --> 00:05:31.497
long enough on my on my life

00:05:31.497 --> 00:05:32.565
support system,

00:05:32.565 --> 00:05:33.666
there was no rescue

00:05:33.666 --> 00:05:36.336
I had to self rescue if I had a problem.

00:05:36.602 --> 00:05:37.637
And we thought about that

00:05:37.637 --> 00:05:38.471
for the seven years

00:05:38.471 --> 00:05:39.572
that we spent building the sub.

00:05:39.572 --> 00:05:40.940
And I designed a lot of the

00:05:40.940 --> 00:05:42.575
safety systems myself

00:05:42.575 --> 00:05:44.510
knowing I was going to be in that sub

00:05:44.510 --> 00:05:46.412
terms of how to drop the weights.

00:05:46.412 --> 00:05:47.814
If I was incapacitated,

00:05:47.814 --> 00:05:49.515
the weights would drop themselves over

00:05:49.515 --> 00:05:51.050
after a certain period of time.

00:05:51.050 --> 00:05:52.552
Many, many

00:05:52.552 --> 00:05:54.354
communications beacons

00:05:54.354 --> 00:05:55.988
for when I got back to the surface.

00:05:55.988 --> 00:05:56.923
If I had drifted off

00:05:56.923 --> 00:05:58.591
someplace, I'd have been picked up

00:05:58.591 --> 00:05:59.425
by satellite.

00:05:59.425 --> 00:06:01.294
They would have reported my position.

00:06:01.294 --> 00:06:03.830
I had radio, I had visual beacons.

00:06:04.230 --> 00:06:05.365
It wouldn't have required

00:06:05.365 --> 00:06:06.666
an aircraft search.

00:06:06.666 --> 00:06:08.868
They'd have found me pretty quickly.

00:06:08.868 --> 00:06:11.003
So you think all that stuff through,

00:06:11.003 --> 00:06:12.905
but the feeling in the moment,

00:06:12.905 --> 00:06:14.874
it's almost a sacred space.

00:06:14.874 --> 00:06:16.008
It's a place where there's

00:06:16.008 --> 00:06:17.343
nobody else there.

00:06:17.343 --> 00:06:18.177
It's just you,

00:06:18.177 --> 00:06:20.012
you and yourself and

00:06:20.012 --> 00:06:22.181
and a sense of deep time.

00:06:22.181 --> 00:06:23.216
You're looking at something

00:06:23.216 --> 00:06:24.584
that no one's ever seen.

00:06:24.584 --> 00:06:26.586
It's been that way for

00:06:26.586 --> 00:06:27.286
hundreds of millions,

00:06:27.286 --> 00:06:28.921
if not billions of years

00:06:28.921 --> 00:06:30.723
at Titan like it's different.

00:06:30.723 --> 00:06:33.059
You feel this the presence

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:35.261
of the tragedy,

00:06:35.261 --> 00:06:36.763
you know, and I think that's the lure.

00:06:36.763 --> 00:06:38.297
I think that's why people want to go

00:06:38.297 --> 00:06:39.999
and experience it for themselves.

00:06:39.999 --> 00:06:42.335
To feel, to remember history.

00:06:42.702 --> 00:06:43.503
You know,

00:06:43.503 --> 00:06:45.271
I think people go to battlefields,

00:06:45.271 --> 00:06:47.740
to Gettysburg, to, you know,

00:06:47.740 --> 00:06:49.242
Normandy Beach

00:06:49.242 --> 00:06:49.976
and all those things

00:06:49.976 --> 00:06:51.010
to remember history

00:06:51.010 --> 00:06:51.911
and to take it in

00:06:51.911 --> 00:06:53.446
and make it make it part of their lives.

00:06:53.446 --> 00:06:55.014
I don't think that

00:06:55.014 --> 00:06:55.381
you know,

00:06:55.381 --> 00:06:55.681
I don't think

00:06:55.681 --> 00:06:56.916
there's anything wrong with that.

00:06:56.916 --> 00:06:57.583
I mean, some people

00:06:57.583 --> 00:06:59.252
don't like they say it's a gravesite

00:06:59.252 --> 00:07:00.253
and that sort of thing.

00:07:00.253 --> 00:07:01.120
But I think it's important

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:02.155
for us to remember.

00:07:02.155 --> 00:07:04.957
But here's a case starkly today

00:07:05.024 --> 00:07:06.559
where the collective

00:07:06.559 --> 00:07:09.061
we didn't remember the lesson of Titanic

00:07:09.061 --> 00:07:11.230
these guys at Ocean Gate didn't

00:07:11.230 --> 00:07:12.031
because the

00:07:12.031 --> 00:07:12.732
the arrogance

00:07:12.732 --> 00:07:13.466
and the hubris

00:07:13.466 --> 00:07:15.601
that sent that ship to its doom

00:07:15.601 --> 00:07:16.969
is exactly the same thing

00:07:16.969 --> 00:07:18.604
that sent those people in that

00:07:18.604 --> 00:07:20.239
that sub to their fate.

00:07:20.239 --> 00:07:21.808
And I just think it's heartbreaking.

00:07:21.808 --> 00:07:23.843
I think it's heartbreaking that it was

00:07:23.910 --> 00:07:25.144
it was so preventable.

00:07:28.481 --> 00:07:29.348
James Cameron, I'm sorry

00:07:29.348 --> 00:07:30.650
we're talking in these circumstances,

00:07:30.650 --> 00:07:32.618
but I appreciate the level of detail

00:07:32.618 --> 00:07:33.186
and expertize

00:07:33.186 --> 00:07:34.287
that you bring to the conversation.

00:07:34.287 --> 00:07:36.088
So thank you very much.

00:07:36.088 --> 00:07:37.123
Well, thank you, Anderson.

00:07:37.123 --> 00:07:38.624
It's been a pleasure.

00:07:40.393 --> 00:07:40.693
All right.

00:07:40.693 --> 00:07:41.227
Wish you well.

